+# Native commit intent and restoration acknowledgement
+
+This source extension adds `rotation_native_intent` and `rotation_native_ack` to the existing `native_v1` reservation contract. It does not expose a native close route, submit a model turn, or make a serialized claim authoritative.
+
+## Commit intent
+
+The request contains v, session_id, epoch, claim_id, token, host_claim_id and request_sha256. The digest binds the host's immutable claim-request bytes. All issued identities must match the native claim. The first intent requires an unexpired claim, unchanged Relay-owned Codex enrollment and the exact current completed-turn evidence. The ledger persists one immutable commit-requested intent. Exact repeated requests return its current descriptive state; they never replay owner work.
+
+An unresolved native intent excludes another legacy or native claim even after reservation expiry. It also fences source identity changes through session_bind and keeps capsule metadata under the existing fencing rule. Telemetry remains recordable. The default status view prioritizes unresolved native intent and then retirement ACK, regardless of later signals.
+
+## Restoration acknowledgement
+
+The host must first obtain and verify the actual private native owner's synchronized restored journal record, including runtime/process incarnation and original capsule bytes. The ledger validates the record's structural binding; it cannot inspect a native process or turn caller JSON into owner authority.
+
+The ACK request adds receipt to the same intent identity and request digest. Receipt uses the native journal's exact fields: v, state, operation_id, claim_id, capsule_sha256, source, destination_thread_id, settings_sha256 and restored. The source receipt is the core's snake_case receipt with an unsigned numeric admission_generation; it is matched explicitly against the earlier app-server receipt with camelCase fields and a decimal generation string. It is not the app-server reservation wrapper.
+
+Operation and host-claim UUIDs, capsule digest, source thread/runtime/turn/rollout/generation, distinct destination thread/runtime/rollout and restored capsule/settings digests must match. Process ID must be a positive bounded integer; its actual incarnation is verified by the host's owner route, since CLI and app-server processes need not have the same PID.
+
+ACK needs no new model turn and may complete after the preparation lease expires. Within the same TaskLedger transaction, the destination must already be registered as Relay-owned Codex in the same cwd/logical task with its exact restored transcript path. No other source may have acknowledged that destination. Registration is separate host work after native proof; ACK does not fabricate a destination registration.
+
+The ledger atomically records the exact native receipt and destination, closes the source claim and marks the intent acknowledged. Exact duplicate ACK is descriptive/idempotent; conflicting receipt or destination is refused. WAL failure preserves all previous in-memory state.
+
+## Release and recovery
+
+A fully released native reservation with no intent/ACK disappears only from the default availability view. Explicit epoch status retains the complete released claim. An expired but unreleased claim remains visible. This lets existing policy/preparation checks evaluate after a genuine release without weakening their null-claim rule.
+
+This extension provides no expiry-based abort or abandonment of native intent. A lost or uncertain commit remains fenced until actual restoration evidence is acknowledged or a future owner-verified terminal refusal/cancellation protocol proves the old request cannot seal. Absence of a journal file is insufficient. The foreground commit route and that terminal refusal protocol remain required before automatic execution is enabled.
